<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pontius Pilate</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 26/27–36/37 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Pontius Pilatus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pontius Pilate was the fifth Roman governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judaea</a>, serving under Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> from 26/27 to 36/37 CE. The gospels, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> attribute the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crucifixion-of-jesus">crucifixion of Jesus</a> to his orders, and the gospels portray him as reluctant to carry it out. Josephus and Philo record several conflicts with the Jewish population, and Josephus reports that he was dismissed after violently putting down a Samaritan uprising at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-gerizim">Mount Gerizim</a>. Little survives of his rule beyond his coins and the Pilate Stone inscription, and his fate after leaving office is unknown. Historians are divided on whether he governed brutally and ineptly or with moderate competence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pontius-pilate.jpg" alt="Pontius Pilate" width="330" height="253" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antonio Ciseri · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ecce_homo_by_Antonio_Ciseri_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…that he was born around 6–4 BCE, that he was baptised by John the Baptist, that he taught and gathered disciples in Galilee, proclaiming the coming kingdom of God, and that he was crucified in Jerusalem around 30 CE on the orders of the Roman governor <b>Pontius Pilate</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
